<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jugurtha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 160–104 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jugurtha was king of Numidia, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> who seized the whole kingdom after killing one adoptive brother and fighting the other. His massacre of Italian traders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> in 112 BCE led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which exposed corruption in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>. Betrayed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> in 105 BCE by his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> of Mauretania, he was led in Marius's triumph and died in prison in Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugurtha.jpg" alt="Jugurtha" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faustus_Cornelius_Sulla,_AR_denarius,_56_BC,_RRC_426-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Numidia lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> roughly within what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. Masinissa, chief of the Massyli near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, used Roman support in the Second Punic War to found a kingdom, and on his death in 148 BCE his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> succeeded him. Jugurtha, whose name probably means he exceeded them in Libyco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, was Micipsa's adopted son and the illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastanabal">Mastanabal</a>, which made him an illegitimate grandson of Masinissa.</p><p>Jugurtha was so popular among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> to assist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, in 134 and 133 BCE, he served alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> could be bribed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> has him call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> a city for sale and doomed to quick destruction, if it should find a buyer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> died in 118 BCE, Jugurtha shared the succession with Micipsa's sons Hiempsal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a>. Jugurtha quarrelled with Hiempsal at once and had him killed, which led to open war with Adherbal. Adherbal fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after being defeated, and Roman officials divided Numidia, probably in 116, giving Jugurtha the western half, though accusations arose that they had taken bribes.</p><p>By 112 BCE Jugurtha again attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and penned him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>. Roman residents there urged Adherbal to hold out, but the Senate, with its troops busy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a>, only sent embassies. When Cirta fell, Jugurtha's troops massacred many of its inhabitants, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sent the consul Lucius Calpurnius Bestia. A favourable peace again raised suspicions of bribery. Jugurtha was summoned to Rome to give evidence, but a tribune's veto prevented it.</p><p>War resumed under the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-numidicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Numidicus</a>. In 110 BCE Jugurtha forced the capitulation of an entire army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-postumius-albinus">Aulus Postumius Albinus</a>. Metellus won several battles in 109 BCE without ending the war, and Marius, once elected consul for 107 BCE, took command against Jugurtha's guerrilla warfare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jugurtha was allied by marriage with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> of Mauretania, who was his father-in-law and joined the fighting against Marius in 107 BCE. In 105 BCE Marius sent his quaestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> to Mauretania. Bocchus agreed to hand Jugurtha over in exchange for land in western Numidia as far as the Mulucha River. Jugurtha was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in chains and paraded in Marius's triumph, where his robes were removed and an earring was torn off, costing him part of an ear. He was thrown into the Tullianum prison and died there in 104 BCE, most likely by strangulation, though he may have been starved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jugurtha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jugurthine War of 112–105 BCE showed the strengths of Numidian warfare: <b>Jugurtha</b> bribed Roman commanders, forced an army under Aulus Postumius Albinus to pass under the yoke near Suthul in 110 BCE, fought Quintus Caecilius Metellus at the Battle of the Muthul in 109 BCE and then…</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They served as cavalry for <b>Jugurtha</b> and for Julius Caesar, and some received Roman citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaetuli">Gaetuli</a></li><li><blockquote>Bocchus I handed <b>Jugurtha</b> of Numidia to Sulla in 105 BCE, gaining western Numidia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li><li><blockquote>Masinissa of Numidia seized the Emporia in the 160s BCE, and Leptis later allied with Rome against <b>Jugurtha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.37,6.61&amp;year=-124" aria-label="Cirta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4716 852 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jugurtha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.37,6.61&amp;year=-124">What was here in 124 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A city for sale and doomed to speedy destruction if it finds a purchaser!&#8221;</p><footer>Sallust, The Jugurthine War, 35 &#183; tr. J. C. Rolfe, 1921 &#183; of Rome, as he looked back on leaving it, as Sallust reports &#183; <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Jugurthinum/1*.html" rel="noopener">penelope.uchicago.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
